Real code:

public interface FileSystemItem {
    long getSize();
}

public class File implements FileSystemItem {
    private long size;
    public File(String name, long size) { this.size = size; }
    @Override
    public long getSize() { return size; }
}

File.getSize() just returns its own stored size field directly, no loop, no recursion. What role does this play in the tree structure?,"It's the ""leaf"" case — the simplest, base-case implementation that terminates recursion immediately with a direct answer, rather than delegating to any children",It's irrelevant to the tree structure since File has no children field,"It's the ""container"" case, since it implements the shared interface","It's a Proxy for Folder, since both implement FileSystemItem",,1,"File is the base case of the recursive structure — it answers directly with no delegation, terminating any recursive chain that reaches it.",1

Real code:

public class Folder implements FileSystemItem {
    private List<FileSystemItem> children;
    public void add(FileSystemItem item) { children.add(item); }
    @Override
    public long getSize() {
        long total = 0;
        for (FileSystemItem child : children) {
            total += child.getSize();
        }
        return total;
    }
}

Why does Folder.getSize() never need an if (child instanceof File) or if (child instanceof Folder) check inside its loop?,"Because Folder can only ever contain File objects, never other Folder objects","Because every child in the list satisfies FileSystemItem, and calling child.getSize() dispatches polymorphically to whichever concrete class's implementation actually applies — the loop's code stays identical regardless of what kind of child it's currently processing",Because getSize() is a static method shared across all implementers,Because Java automatically inserts type checks into every loop over an interface type,,2,Polymorphic dispatch through the shared interface is exactly what removes the need for any type-checking inside the loop.,1

Given:

Folder root = new Folder("root");
File a = new File("a.txt", 10);
root.add(a);
System.out.println(root.getSize());

What does this print, and why?,0 — since File doesn't implement getSize() correctly,It throws a NullPointerException since children was never initialized,"10 — root's loop runs once, calling a.getSize(), which returns 10 directly (base case), and root's running total starts at 0 and adds that 10",20 — getSize() doubles the value for auditing purposes,,3,"A straightforward trace: one child, base case returns its own size, total accumulates to 10.",1

Now extend it:

Folder root = new Folder("root");
File a = new File("a.txt", 10);
Folder sub = new Folder("sub");
File b = new File("b.txt", 5);
sub.add(b);
root.add(a);
root.add(sub);
System.out.println(root.getSize());

What does root.getSize() return?,10 — since Folders inside Folders are ignored by getSize(),It throws a ClassCastException since sub isn't a File,5 — only the innermost file's size is counted,"15 — root's loop processes a (returns 10 directly) and sub (which is itself a Folder, so calling sub.getSize() triggers a fresh loop over sub's own children, returning 5 from b), summing to 10 + 5 = 15",,4,"Tracing the nested call: root's loop delegates to sub's own getSize() invocation, which itself sums its own children before returning up to root's total.",1

Real code (org chart):

public class Manager implements Employee {
    private List<Employee> directReports;
    public void addDirectReport(Employee employee) { directReports.add(employee); }
    @Override
    public double getTotalSalary() {
        double total = salary;
        for (Employee report : directReports) {
            total += report.getTotalSalary();
        }
        return total;
    }
}

Notice Manager.getTotalSalary() starts total at salary (the manager's OWN salary), not at 0. Why does this differ from Folder.getSize(), which starts its total at 0?,"Manager and Folder are actually identical in this respect, and the difference is a typo","A Manager is itself a salaried employee whose own compensation should count toward the total budget, unlike a Folder, which has no size of its own beyond what its contents add up to — the starting value reflects whether the container itself contributes a base value on top of its children","Employee interfaces require starting at salary, unlike FileSystemItem",This is a bug — Manager.getTotalSalary() should start at 0 just like Folder.getSize(),,2,"Whether a container contributes its own base value is a domain-specific design choice, not a rule of the pattern itself — Manager legitimately needs it, Folder legitimately doesn't.",1
